  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nFriday:\nBehind the frontal passage, low-level moisture remains trapped\nbelow a sharp inversion. This may allow for drizzle and low-\nlevel clouds to persist through Friday. The longer the\nclouds/drizzle linger, the cooler high temperatures reach on\nFriday afternoon. Currently, highs are only progged to reach\ninto the 50s, though may end up a bit cooler. By Friday night,\nclouds dissipate as light north winds settle into the area. This\nwill set the stage for a cold night, with lows falling into the\n30s. Recent trends would suggest increasing confidence in\nwidespread frost, with a freeze likely in the eastern Ozarks. A\nfreeze remains possible in other parts of the area, particularly \nin the sheltered valleys. Frost and freeze headlines will be \nneeded in subsequent forecasts. However, it is plausible low- \nlevel cloud cover inhibits frost/freeze concerns for Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, that will need monitored closely \nover the next 24 to 36 hours.\n\nSaturday-Sunday:\nCooler temperatures persist on Saturday with highs in the middle\n50s. This will be accompanied by dry weather. Clearing skies \ninto Saturday night set the stage for additional frost/freeze \nconcerns. \n\nA bit warmer into the lower/middle 60s on Sunday, before yet\nanother chilly night into Monday morning. This will likely be\nthe last potential morning for frost/freeze with a warming trend\nexpected through mid next week.\n\n\nEarly Next Week:\nEnsemble guidance are slowly coming into better agreement into\nnext week, with a ridge of high pressure overspreading the\ncentral CONUS. This will support increasing mid-level heights,\nand thus a warming trend. Temperatures are progged to be 5 to 10\ndegrees above normal, with daily highs in the upper 60s to lower\n70s. This will be accompanied by another dry stretch of weather,\nfurther amplifying drought conditions and potential Fall fire\nweather conditions.",
